        <div id='metabolic_pathways_content'>
        
  <div class="mp_text_box">
    <h3>THE MAIZECYC PIPELINE</h3>
    <p>
      MaizeCyc 2.0.2 pathways were computationally inferred using Pathway Tools 
      software (SRI\) (Monaco et al, Plant Genome, 2013\)
    </p>
    <br>
    <p>
      SOURCES OF FUNCTIONAL ANNOTATIONS INCLUDE:
      <ul class="mp">
        <li>Classical Maize Genes mapped to FGS</li>
        <li>MaizeSequence.org (coordinates, cross-references, etc.\)</li>
        <li>MaizeGDB (locus names/ synonyms, molecular function, etc\)</li>
        <li>UniProtKB/SwissProt (functional descriptions and EC #\)</li>
        <li>Gene Ontology (mol. function, biol. process, cellular location\)</li>
        <li>Proteomics-supported gene annotations (e.g., cellular location\)</li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <br>
    <p>
      DATA CURATION 
      <ul class="mp">
        <li>772 literature-based GO-term annotations (MaizeGDB\)</li>
        <li>Carotenoid, flavonoid, and flavonol biosynthesis pathways </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <br>
    <p>
      <h3>THE CORNCYC PIPELINE</h3>
      CornCyc 2.0 is based on the Ensemble Enzyme Prediction Pipeline (E2P2 v1.0\) 
      (Lee Chae and Seung Y. Rhee, unpublished\)
      <ul class="mp">
        <li>
          Uses an averaged weighted integration algorithm based on BLAST / 
          CatFam / Priam results
          <ul class="mp" style="margin-left:10px">
            <li>
              BLAST: e-value cutoff <= 1e-30, subset of SwissProt 15.3 
            </li>
            <li>
              CatFam: version 2.0, 1% FDR profile library
            </li>
            <li>
              Priam: Release November 2010 
            </li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
          Weights were determined via a 0.632 bootstrap process over 1000 rounds 
          of testing based on experimentally assigned functional annotation
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <br>
    <p>
      DATA CURATION
      <ul class="mp">
        <li>Auxin, brassinosteroid, gibberellin pathways (MaizeGDB\)</li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <br>
    <p>
      <h3>COMPARING MAIZECYC AND CORNCYC STATISTICS</h3>
      <table>
        <tr>
          <th width=20"></td>
          <th></th>
          <th width=4></th>
          <th bgcolor="#e0e0e0">MAIZECYC</th>
          <th bgcolor="#e0e0e0">CORNCYC</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td></td>
          <td>Transport Rxns</td>
          <td></td>
          <td>68</td>
          <td>24</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td></td>
          <td>Transporters</td>
          <td></td>
          <td>291</td>
          <td>175</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td></td>
          <td>Proteins</td>
          <td></td>
          <td>39,655</td>
          <td>10,511</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td></td>
          <td>Enzymes</td>
          <td></td>
          <td>8,889</td>
          <td>10,478</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td></td>
          <td>Enzymatic Rxns</td>
          <td></td>
          <td>2,110</td>
          <td>2,081</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td></td>
          <td>Pathways</td>
          <td></td>
          <td>391</td>
          <td>334</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td></td>
          <td>Genes</td>
          <td></td>
          <td>39,655</td>
          <td>6575 (splicing\)</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
      <ul class="mp">
        <li>
          More transporter proteins and reactions in MaizeCyc
        </li>
        <li>
          No alternative splicing in MaizeCyc; CornCyc has alternatively 
          spliced protein variants (~4,000 variants\)
        </li>
        <li>
          Fewer genes in CornCyc (lower coverage\)
        </li>
        <li>
          More (spliced variant\) enzymes are assigned to fewer reactions and 
          pathways in CornCyc [high stringency/lower coverage] (Corollary: 
          Fewer enzymes are assigned to more reactions and pathways in MaizeCyc\)
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <br>
    <p>
      <h3>PERFORMANCE TESTING</h3>
      A set of 197 UniProt proteins with experimental evidence and alignment 
      to B73 RefGen_v2 FGS protein models are used for performance testing
    </p>
    <br>
    <p>
      PERFORMANCE MEASURES
      <ul class="mp">
        <li>
          TP (True Positive\): perfect gene model-EC match
        </li>
        <li>
          FP (False Positive\): EC mismatch for a given gene model 
        </li>
        <li>
          FN  (False Negative\): missing gene be multiple Uniprot IDs associated 
          with the same gene model, and multiple EC for the same gene model\)
        </li>
        <li>
          TN (True Negative\) not defined: pairs missing in the Uniprot set as 
          well as in the database  
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <br>
    <p>
      DEFINITIONS<br>
      Precision (P\) = TP / (TP +FP\)<br>
      Recall (R\)  = TP  / (TP+FN\)<br>
      F-measure =  2 * (P * R\) /  (P + R\)<br>
    </p>
    <br>
    <p>
      RESULTS<br>
      MaizeCyc<br>
      Precision = 0.711<br>
      Recall = 0.245<br>
      F = 0.365<br>
    </p>
    <br>
    <p>
      CornCyc<br>
      Precision = 0.873<br>
      Recall = 0.913<br>
      F = 0.893<br>
    </p>
    <br>
    <p>
      <h3>CONCLUSION</h3>
      MaizeCyc and CornCyc are complementary. While MaizeCyc is a higher 
      coverage in terms of assigned enzymatic reactions and pathways, CornCyc, 
      which includes spliced variants, is a higher stringency resource in 
      assigning functions to enzymes.  
    </p>
      
        </div>
      </div>
    <td>
  </tr>
</table>
)



